### quote
short: None
long: Some("quote")
visible_aliases: []
hidden_aliases: []
short_aliases: None
index: None
action: Set
num_args: Some(0..=1)
value_type: jp_cli::cli::flag::Word<bool>
possible_values: ["true", "false"]
value_names: Some(["BOOL"])
value_delimiter: None
default_values: []
help_heading: None
conflicts: []
set: []
help: Some("Pre-fill the editor with the last assistant message quoted as a markdown blockquote (each line prefixed with ` >  `)")
long_help: Some("Pre-fill the editor with the last assistant message quoted as a markdown blockquote (each line prefixed with ` >  `).\n\nUseful for inline replies: open `$EDITOR` with the assistant's last response pre-quoted, then intersperse your replies between the quoted lines (mutt/email style). The complete buffer — quotes plus your replies — becomes your next message.\n\n`--quote=false` seeds the message verbatim, without the ` >  ` prefixes. `--quote=true` is the same as a bare `--quote`. Both values also work unattached (`--quote false`); any other word after `--quote` stays part of the query, so `jp q --quote what now?` still asks \"what now?\". To ask a question that *is* `true` or `false`, put it after `--`.\n\nForces the editor open by default; respects `--no-edit` / `--edit=false` if explicitly suppressed, in which case the quoted text is sent as-is and echoed to the terminal before the turn runs. Composes with `--replay`: the quote is taken from the stream *after* the replayed turn has been trimmed, i.e. the assistant message preceding the turn being replayed.\n\nIf no prior assistant message exists in this conversation, a warning is emitted and the editor opens with whatever other content was seeded (query, stdin, or empty).")

